Finding: {"title":"emergency-password-reset does not revoke existing sessions or force a password change","severity":"HIGH","area":"Session management / incident response","files":["src/server/routes/routes-auth.ts"],"detail":"POST /emergency-password-reset (routes-auth.ts:1547-1588) is explicitly documented as 'admin-triggered, bypasses email' for emergency use, and hashes+writes the new password directly to authAccount. It does NOT call auth.api.revokeUserSessions (unlike grant-super-admin at line 1356 and create-user-temp-password at line 730, which both explicitly revoke sessions for exactly this reason) and does not set mustChangePassword: true.","impact":"If the reason for an 'emergency' reset is a compromised account (the primary use case for this endpoint), an attacker who already holds a valid session cookie/token for that account keeps full access after the reset — the emergency reset does not actually evict them. This defeats the endpoint's stated purpose.","recommendation":"After the password update, call auth.api.revokeUserSessions({ body: { userId } }) (non-fatal try/catch, matching the pattern used elsewhere in this file) and set mustChangePassword: true so the user must re-authenticate with the new credential on next use.","confidence":"high"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

